<script>
import TargetLog from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.abide'
import targetLogSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import targetLogTestSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.test.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import listTargetsSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/listTargets.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }
import setTargetSource from 'crm/src/pipeline/setTarget.handler.ts' with { type: 'text' }

import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Registry from '#src/lib/Registry.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="A `database` keeps what a restart would lose">
        <Example
            id="database"
            app="crm"
            title={"`database(name, { migrations })` — the server's own SQLite file"}
            summary="Every target the team has set, read from a file on the server. Set one, and the list still holds it after a restart."
            serverFiles={['src/pipeline/lib/crmDatabase.server.ts']}
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.abide': targetLogSource,
                'src/pipeline/listTargets.handler.ts': listTargetsSource,
                'src/pipeline/setTarget.handler.ts': setTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/TargetLog.test.ts': targetLogTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TargetLog />
        </Example>

        <p><code>database</code>, from <code>@abide/abide/server</code>, hands back a function. Its
            first call opens <code>crm.sqlite</code> in <code>APP_DATA_DIR</code>, and every call
            after hands back the same <code>bun:sqlite</code> <code>Database</code>, so a handler
            writes plain SQL against it.</p>

        <p>Each migration runs once, in order, and the file remembers how many it has run. A new
            column is one more string at the end of the list, never an edit to one already there.
            A migration can be a function too, for rows a first boot seeds, and it can seed through
            the app's own helpers: the opener called inside it hands back the database it is
            migrating. If one throws, none of
            that boot's migrations are kept. A file a newer build migrated is refused rather than
            opened, unless the opener says <code>{'{ openNewer: true }'}</code>: then it opens as it
            stands, runs nothing and keeps its count, so switching back from a branch that added a
            migration still opens the file.</p>

        <p>In place of a name, <code>database</code> takes a path to the file, or
            <code>':memory:'</code>, or a function handing back either. Anything holding a
            <code>/</code> or a <code>.</code> is a path, so <code>'todos.sqlite'</code> is that
            file, and a relative one starts from the working directory, as it would anywhere else. The function is called as the file opens, so
            <code>{'database(() => config().TODOS_DB)'}</code> opens wherever the running app is
            configured to keep it.</p>

        <p>An app that made its file before it used <code>database</code> points at it where it
            is, and writes its first migration with <code>CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS</code>. The
            file has run no migration yet, so the first one runs over tables already there and
            keeps their rows, and every one after it runs as usual.</p>

        <p>Under <code>bun test</code> each test file opens each database empty, in a scratch
            directory of its own, whatever it names, so a test never writes the file the app serves
            and never reads another test file's rows. Two openers of one file share it, as a second
            connection does, and the function is still called, so a check inside it still throws. The
            app closes the file as it stops, and the next call opens it again as it was.
            <code>close()</code> does the same by hand, and <code>{'close({ remove: true })'}</code>
            deletes the file the opener last opened, even after the app's stop closed it, so the next call
            opens it empty.</p>

        <p>A store and the app's own tables share one file when <code>store.sqlite</code> is handed
            the opener itself: <code>{"store.sqlite(crmDatabase, 'target')"}</code>. The store calls
            it at each read and write, so it opens the file again after a stop closed it. Handed a
            string, <code>store.sqlite</code> opens it as <code>database</code> would, scratch
            directory and all.</p>

        <p><code>open</code> runs at every open, before the migrations, for what a connection sets
            rather than what the file keeps: <code>{"open: (db) => db.run('PRAGMA synchronous = NORMAL')"}</code>.
            <code>strict</code>, <code>readonly</code> and <code>safeIntegers</code> go to
            <code>bun:sqlite</code> as given. A function naming the file is called as the file opens
            and at no other call, so a path that changes takes effect at the next open, after
            <code>close()</code>. A test that points the path somewhere else partway through a file
            calls <code>close()</code> first, or it goes on reading the file it opened. A function
            that hands back anything but a string, such as a config key with no value yet, throws
            rather than open a file named for it.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
